t\n`int` accepts every value (`INT_MIN..=INT_MAX`), so the enum-like out-of-range\ncases are covered by feeding extreme/arbitrary `int` values across FFI\n(rows 12–14). Every row below is implemented in `tests/phase_c_errors.rs`.\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | [ ] |\n|---|----------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `forward_goto_example` | `x < 0` → `goto error` (`x = -1`) | `stderr` gets `\"Error: negative input\\n\"`, `stdout` empty, returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 2 | `forward_goto_example` | `x == INT_MIN` (extreme negative, `-x` overflows) | same as row 1: `-1` + stderr message | [ ] |\n| 3 | `forward_goto_example` | many randomized `x < 0` (seeded) | `-1` + stderr message for every value | [ ] |\n| 4 | `open_with_cleanup` | `fopen` fails, `ENOENT`: path does not exist | `stderr` `\"Error: opening or processing file <path>\\n\"`, no `fclose` (fp is NULL), returns `NULL` | [ ] |\n| 5 | `open_with_cleanup` | `fopen` fails, `ENOENT`: empty string path `\"\"` | as row 4 with `<path>` empty | [ ] |\n| 6 | `open_with_cleanup` | `filename == NULL` (null pointer across FFI) | `fopen(NULL,\"r\")` → `NULL`; `fprintf(\"%s\", NULL)` prints `(null)`; returns `NULL` | [ ] |\n| 7 | `open_with_cleanup` | `fopen` fails, `EACCES`: existing file with mode `000` | as row 4, returns `NULL` | [ ] |\n| 8 | `open_with_cleanup` | `fopen` fails, `ENAMETOOLONG`: oversized path (~5000 bytes, > `PATH_MAX`) | as row 4 (whole oversized name echoed to stderr), returns `NULL` | [ ] |\n| 9 | `open_with_cleanup` | `fopen` fails, `ENOTDIR`: a regular file used as a directory component | as row 4, returns `NULL` | [ ] |\n|10 | `open_with_cleanup` | `fopen` *succeeds* but the read loop sets `ferror` (`EISDIR`: path is a directory) | takes the second `goto cleanup`: stderr message, **`fclose(fp)` is executed**, returns `NULL` | [ ] |\n|11 | `driver` | `forward_goto_example` returned `-1` (`num < 0`) → early `return -1`; the file is never opened | returns `-1`, stderr has only the negative-input message, `stdout` empty, no file error message | [ ] |\n|12 | `driver` | `num < 0` **and** a bad filename (error precedence: first error wins) | returns `-1` (not `-2`); no `\"opening or processing\"` message at all | [ ] |\n|13 | `driver` | `num >= 0`, `open_with_cleanup` returned `NULL` (all of rows 4–10 as filenames) | returns `-2` after printing `Processing:`/`Goto output:` | [ ] |\n|14 | `driver` | `num = INT_MIN` (extreme, negative) / `num = INT_MAX` (extreme, positive, `x*2` overflows) with a bad file | `INT_MIN` → `-1`; `INT_MAX` → `-2` (overflowed `res = -2` is *not* the `-1` sentinel) | [ ] |\n|15 | `driver` | `filename == NULL` with `num >= 0` | returns `-2`, stderr contains `(null)` | [ ] |\n|16 | `open_with_cleanup` | zero-length read source that still opens fine (`/dev/null`, empty file) — **not** an error: must NOT take cleanup | returns non-NULL `FILE*`, no stderr output | [ ] |\n\nRow 16 is the negative control for the error surface: it proves the cleanup\npath is not taken for a successful-but-empty read.\n","userModified":false}}
